---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Guilloche from '../components/ornament/Guilloche.astro';
import Rule from '../components/ornament/Rule.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

// The differentiator, written as a public artefact rather than a trust-page
// formality. No competitor in this space publishes their verification, and
// "here are our tests, read them" is a stronger claim than any adjective.
//
// ─── Why this page has per-vertical sections ────────────────────────────────
//
// It stayed at the ROOT when /verify moved to /finance/verify, and the split is
// deliberate: this page is a CLAIM — why the numbers can be trusted — and that
// claim is genuinely site-wide. /finance/verify is a PROCEDURE, and its procedure
// is five spreadsheet formulas and one SEC calculator, every one of them
// financial. A claim belongs to the site; a procedure belongs to its subject.
//
// The restructure was forced by a real defect: the page opened on "Money is
// never a decimal", "The rounding policy" and a worked verification that is an
// amortisation schedule, so a machinist clicking "How we verify" from the tap
// drill calculator landed on a mortgage example and had no reason to read on.
//
// The route in was NOT what it looked like, and the difference changed the fix.
// No machining page links here from its own content — checked, and the string
// "methodology" does not appear anywhere under src/pages/machining/. The only
// inbound links are the masthead and footer in BaseLayout, which every page on
// the site carries. So there was no per-page link to repoint; the layout's two
// links are now vertical-aware instead, which is only possible because
// `vertical` became a required prop.
//
// The shared claim now comes first, then each vertical gets its own section
// under a STABLE id (#finance, #machining) so the pages in that vertical can
// link straight to the part that concerns them.

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE.url}/` },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: 'Methodology',
        item: `${SITE.url}/methodology`,
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  vertical="site"
  title="How we verify our numbers | QuickOper"
  description="Every calculation on QuickOper is a pure function with fixture tests checked against published third-party figures. The formulas, the rounding policy, the test cases and their sources — all public."
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <article class="text-ink-soft space-y-4">
    <div class="relative">
      <Guilloche
        size={230}
        class="text-brand pointer-events-none absolute -top-12 -right-10 hidden opacity-[0.16] sm:block"
      />
      <h1 class="text-ink relative text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        How we verify our numbers
      </h1>
    </div>

    <p class="text-lg">
      A calculator is only worth using if its arithmetic is right, and almost nobody
      publishing one will show you how they checked. This page shows you.
    </p>

    <p class="text-ink-mute text-sm">
      What follows applies to everything here. For the specifics of one subject:{' '}
      <a class="text-brand underline" href="#finance">money</a> or{' '}
      <a class="text-brand underline" href="#machining">the shop floor</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-ink pt-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
      The calculation is separate from the interface
    </h2>

    <p>
      Every calculation lives in a pure function that knows nothing about the page it ends
      up on — no access to the document, no framework, no interface code. That is not
      tidiness for its own sake. It means the arithmetic can be tested directly,
      exhaustively, and without a browser, so a bug has nowhere to hide behind a rendering
      quirk.
    </p>

    <p>
      Those functions are tested against <strong class="text-ink">fixtures</strong>: a set
      of inputs with a known-correct output, where the source of that output is written
      into the test as a comment. A test that only compares our code against our own
      expectations proves the code is self-consistent, not that it is correct. The tests
      that matter check us against something external.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-ink pt-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
      What we will not build
    </h2>

    <p>
      There is a rule here that costs us traffic and we keep anyway:{' '}
      <strong class="text-ink">
        if verifying an answer needs expertise we do not have, we do not build the tool.
      </strong>
    </p>

    <p>
      That rules out tax withholding, payroll, capital gains, equity compensation and
      anything requiring a tax table — categories with far more search traffic than the
      ones we do build. A calculator whose output nobody here could check is a calculator
      that can be confidently, consistently wrong without anyone noticing, and that is the
      one failure this site cannot survive.
    </p>

    <Rule class="pt-4" />

    <h2 class="text-ink pt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Read the tests</h2>

    <p>
      The source and every test are public at{' '}
      <a class="text-brand underline" href={SITE.repo} rel="noopener">{SITE.repo}</a>. The
      calculation modules are under <code>src/lib/calc/</code> and their fixtures under{
        ' '
      }
      <code>tests/calc/</code>, with the source of each expected value cited in a comment
      directly above it.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong class="text-ink"
        >If you find a figure that disagrees with your lender</strong
      >
      — or with an official worked example — please tell us. Open an issue with the source,
      or <a class="text-brand underline" href="/contact">get in touch</a>. Corrections are
      made openly, with the change and its date recorded on the page it affects. That is
      the single most useful thing anyone can send us.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-ink pt-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
      What this does not make us
    </h2>

    <p>
      Verified arithmetic is not financial advice, and we are careful not to blur the two.
      QuickOper works out what the numbers do. It does not know your circumstances and
      does not tell you what to do about them. See{' '}
      <a class="text-brand underline" href="/about">about</a> for what that means in practice.
    </p>

    <Rule class="pt-6" />

    <section id="finance" class="scroll-mt-6 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-ink pt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        Money — how the finance figures are checked
      </h2>

      <p>
        Every calculation below is checked against something that was not written here: a
        spreadsheet function, a published schedule, or a regulator's own worked example.{
          ' '
        }
        <a class="text-brand underline" href="/finance/verify"
          >Check our arithmetic yourself</a
        > is the step-by-step version — five formulas you can paste into Excel or Google Sheets,
        and one calculator run by the SEC.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-ink pt-4 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        A worked verification you can repeat
      </h3>

      <p>
        The debt payoff engine is checked against the standard loan payment formula, which
        every lender, textbook and amortisation table produces the same answer from:
      </p>

      <p class="numeric text-ink bg-sunken border-line rounded-control border p-3">
        P = B × i ÷ (1 − (1 + i)<sup>−n</sup>)
      </p>

      <p>
        For a $10,000 balance at 6.00% annual interest over 60 months, i = 0.005 and the
        formula gives a monthly payment of $193.3280 — the $193.33 that every amortisation
        table quotes. Feed that payment back into our engine and it must clear the balance
        in exactly 60 months.
      </p>

      <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="border-line w-full border-collapse border text-sm">
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="border-line border p-2">Months to clear, from our engine</td>
              <td class="numeric border-line border p-2 text-right">60</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="border-line border p-2">Total interest, from our engine</td>
              <td class="numeric border-line border p-2 text-right">$1,599.68</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="border-line border p-2"
                >Total interest, from the closed formula</td
              >
              <td class="numeric border-line border p-2 text-right">$1,599.68</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="border-line border p-2"><strong>Difference</strong></td>
              <td class="numeric border-line border p-2 text-right"
                ><strong>$0.00</strong></td
              >
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        That test asserts <em>exact</em> equality, not "close enough". An earlier draft allowed
        fifty cents of tolerance; measuring the real figure showed the tolerance was fifty times
        wider than reality and would have quietly absorbed a genuine regression in the rounding
        policy. It was tightened.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-ink pt-4 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        Money is never a decimal
      </h3>

      <p>
        Computers store decimal fractions approximately. In JavaScript,{' '}
        <code class="numeric">0.1 + 0.2</code> is{' '}
        <code class="numeric">0.30000000000000004</code>, and{' '}
        <code class="numeric">1.005 × 100</code> is{' '}
        <code class="numeric">100.49999999999999</code> — so the obvious way to round $1.005
        to the cent gives $1.00 instead of $1.01. That single defect is responsible for a large
        share of the one-cent discrepancies people notice in financial software.
      </p>

      <p>
        Every amount here is stored as a whole number of cents or pence, never as a
        decimal. Where a fraction is unavoidable — interest is{' '}
        <code class="numeric">balance × rate ÷ 12</code>, which rarely lands on a whole
        cent — the rounding is explicit and tested at the boundary in both directions.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-ink pt-4 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        The rounding policy
      </h3>

      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Rounded every period, not at the end.</strong> A statement
          rounds to the cent each month and then carries that figure forward. Computing at full
          precision and rounding once at the end produces a schedule that drifts from the one
          your lender sends.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Half away from zero</strong> on the smallest unit, the convention
          in retail lending, unless a cited source specifies otherwise.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">The final payment absorbs the difference.</strong> It is
          reduced to exactly what remains rather than taking the full amount, so a schedule
          never overshoots and its rows always sum to its total.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <Rule class="pt-6" />

    <section id="machining" class="scroll-mt-6 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-ink pt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        Shop floor — how the machining figures are checked
      </h2>

      <p>
        Same discipline, different failure. A wrong money figure costs someone a decision;
        a wrong tap drill scraps the part, or holds through assembly and lets go in
        service. So the anchor here is a <strong class="text-ink"
          >published tap drill table</strong
        > — ISO 2306 / DIN 336, reproduced identically in the free catalogues of the Emuge,
        Guhring and OSG tap manufacturers. Three independent publishers of the same table, none
        of them us.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-ink pt-4 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        A worked verification you can repeat
      </h3>

      <p>
        Take M8 × 1.25, the row that catches everyone. The shop rule targets 76.98 %
        thread engagement, which asks for a drill of 6.75 mm. No such drill exists in the
        standard series; the published tap drill is <strong class="text-ink"
          >6.8 mm</strong
        >, in every catalogue. Open any of the three and check that.
      </p>

      <p>
        The consequence is the figure this tool exists to report: a 6.8 mm drill in an M8
        × 1.25 hole gives <strong class="text-ink">73.90 %</strong> engagement, not the 76.98
        % that was asked for and not the round 75 % people assume. Both numbers are shown, because
        the difference between the engagement you requested and the engagement your drill actually
        produces is the whole point.
      </p>

      <p>
        6.750 mm is also an <em>exact tie</em> between the 6.7 and 6.8 drills, and half-even
        resolves it upward. Rounding to a convenient 0.5 mm step instead — which every shipping
        competitor examined during research does — returns 7.0 mm here, and turns M4 × 0.7 from
        the correct 3.30 mm into 3.50 mm, dropping engagement from 76.98 % to 55.0 %.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-ink pt-4 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        Length is never a decimal either
      </h3>

      <p>
        The money rule applied to geometry: every dimension is stored as a whole number of
        nanometres, never as a decimal millimetre. A drill is 3 300 000 nm, not 3.3 mm.
      </p>

      <p>
        This matters because choosing a drill is an equality-and-ordering problem over a
        fixed catalogue, and <code class="numeric">3.3 === 3.3</code> is not reliable in floating
        point once a value has been converted from inches and back. One inch is{' '}
        <strong class="text-ink">exactly 25 400 000 nm</strong> — a definition from the 1959
        international yard and pound agreement, not a measurement — so inch drills land on exact
        integers too and a mm → inch → mm round trip loses nothing.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-ink pt-4 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        The constants are derived, not typed
      </h3>

      <p>
        ISO 68-1 gives the thread's fundamental triangle height as{' '}
        <code class="numeric">H = (√3 ÷ 2) × P</code>. The engagement constant that
        follows is 3√3⁄4 = 1.2990381…, and <code class="numeric">100 ÷ K</code> is 76.9800 %
        — which is why the shop rule "drill = major − pitch" lands just under 77 %.
      </p>

      <p>
        It is computed from √3 in the code rather than typed as the trade's four-figure
        1.299. That rounding moves the second decimal of the engagement published for
        #8-32, #10-24 and 5/16-18, and it destroys the exact ties that let the drill
        choice reproduce the published chart at all.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="text-ink-mute pt-4 text-sm">Last updated {SITE.legalLastUpdated}.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
